{
  "dom_id": "step-q345710",
  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14195525/assets/9d3a3f70571b0bfca1d4c5251113b915a8061955cb6f48c310d1939571c14e47.png)\n\nConsider a box with a surface area of $432$ square units whose depth $z$ is twice its height $y.$ Find the value of $y$ that maximizes the volume of the box. Do not round the answer.\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}} units",
  "worked_solution": "**Step 1**: We need to find the value of $y$ such that the volume\n\n$$\nV=x\\cdot y\\cdot z=x\\cdot y\\cdot (2y)=2x{y}^{2}\n$$\n\nis the greatest possible value.\n\n**Step 2**: The surface area is $432$ square units. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2xy+2xz+2yz \\\\ 432 & =2xy+2xz+2yz.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the fact that $z=2y,$ we get the constraint equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}432 & =2xy+2x(2y)+2y(2y) \\\\ 432 & =2xy+4xy+4{y}^{2} \\\\ 432 & =6xy+4{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We make one of the variables the subject in the constraint equation:\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{72}{y}-\\frac{2}{3}y\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute the above into the expression $V=2x{y}^{2}$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V(y) & =2{y}^{2}(\\frac{72}{y}-\\frac{2}{3}y) \\\\  & =144y-\\frac{4}{3}{y}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We solve the equation ${V}^{′}(y)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{V}^{′}(y)=144-4{y}^{2},\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}^{′}(y) & =0 \\\\ 144-4{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =36 \\\\ y & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that in the last step, we only considered the positive root because the side length $y$ must be positive.\n\n**Step 5**: We confirm that $y=6$ gives a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{V}^{″}(y)=-8y\\,⟹\\,{V}^{″}(6)=-48<0,\n$$\n\nSince ${V}^{″}(6)$ is negative, we conclude that this is indeed a maximum of $V(y).$\n\nTherefore, the box with the largest possible volume has height $y=6$ units.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
